event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"At its simplest level, the internet is a global \"network of networks.\" It is not a","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" single entity, but a massive collection of private, public, academic, business, and government networks linked by a variety of","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" electronic, wireless, and optical networking technologies. To understand how it works, we have to look at it as a stack","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":", moving from the physical hardware up to the software protocols.\n\n### The Physical Layer: The Hardware\nMany people imagine","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" the internet as something existing in \"the cloud,\" but the cloud is actually composed of massive data centers and thousands of miles","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" of physical cables. \n\nThe backbone of the internet consists of **fiber-optic cables**. These are thin strands of","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" glass that transmit data as pulses of light. These cables run underground and across ocean floors (submarine cables), connecting continents.","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" When you access a website hosted in another country, your data is literally traveling through a cable at the bottom of the ocean","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" at nearly the speed of light.\n\nOnce the data reaches your city or home, it may travel through copper wires (","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"like coaxial cable or phone lines) or via wireless signals (Wi-Fi or 5G). These signals are managed","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" by **routers** and **switches**. A router acts as a traffic cop; it looks at the destination of a","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" piece of data and decides the most efficient path for it to take to reach its destination.\n\n### The Network Layer:","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" IP Addresses and Packets\nSince the internet is so vast, every connected device needs a unique identifier, known as an","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" **IP (Internet Protocol) address**. Think of this as a digital mailing address. Whether it is your smartphone or a","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" massive server in Virginia, every device has an IP address (e.g., `192.0.2","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":".1`) so that other devices know where to send information.\n\nHowever, data is rarely sent as one giant file","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":". If you download a high-resolution photo, the internet doesn't send the image as a single block. Instead","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":", it breaks the data into small chunks called **packets**. \n\nEach packet contains a portion of the data, the","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" sender's IP address, the receiver's IP address, and a sequence number. These packets may take different physical","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" routes to reach the destination. One packet might travel through a router in New York, while another goes through Chicago. Once","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" they all arrive at the destination, the receiving device uses the sequence numbers to reassemble them into the original file.\n\n","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"### The Transport Layer: TCP\nTo ensure that no packets are lost or corrupted during this journey, the internet uses the","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" **Transmission Control Protocol (TCP)**. TCP is the \"manager\" of the connection. Before data is sent, TCP","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" performs a \"three-way handshake\" to establish a connection between the client (your computer) and the server.\n\n","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"As packets arrive, TCP checks to see if any are missing. If a packet is lost due to network congestion or a","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" hardware glitch, TCP requests that the sender transmit that specific packet again. This ensures that the image or webpage you see is","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" complete and error-free.\n\n### The Translation: DNS\nHumans are not good at remembering strings of numbers like ","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"`142.250.190.46`, but we are good at remembering names like ","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"`google.com`. This is where the **Domain Name System (DNS)** comes in.\n\nDNS is essentially the phone","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"book of the internet. When you type a URL into your browser, your computer first contacts a DNS resolver. The resolver","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" looks up the domain name and returns the corresponding IP address of the server where that website is hosted. This happens in milliseconds","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":", allowing your browser to then initiate a connection to the correct physical machine.\n\n### The Application Layer: HTTP\nNow","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" that the physical connection is established and the IP address is known, your browser needs a way to communicate with the server.","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" This is handled by **HTTP (Hypertext Transfer Protocol)**.\n\nHTTP is a request-response protocol. When you","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" click a link, your browser sends an **HTTP Request** (e.g., a \"GET\" request) to","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" the server, essentially saying, \"Please send me the file located at /homepage.html.\"\n\nThe server processes this","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" request and sends back an **HTTP Response**. This response includes a status code (like `200 OK` if","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" the page exists, or `404 Not Found` if it doesn't) and the actual content of the","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" page, usually written in **HTML, CSS, and JavaScript**.\n\n### Summary: The Full Journey\nWhen you type","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" a URL and hit Enter:\n1. **DNS** translates the domain name into an **IP address**.\n2","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":". **TCP** establishes a connection with the server.\n3. Your browser sends an **HTTP request** for the","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" page.\n4. The server breaks the page data into **packets**.\n5. Those packets travel through **routers","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"** and **fiber-optic cables** across the globe.\n6. Your device receives the packets, **TCP**","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" reassembles them, and the browser renders the **HTML** into the visual website you see on your screen.","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

